<section class="inchy-guide__row" id="fullness"><h2>START WITH<br>SATIETY</h2><div>
  <p>Satiety is the feeling of fullness that lasts after eating. GLP-1 is a gut hormone involved in appetite regulation and the body’s response to a meal.</p>
  <p>Protein and certain dietary fibers can stimulate the body’s own GLP-1 release. The studies below explain the evidence. A change in a hormone measurement does not automatically mean less hunger or less food eaten.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="inchy-guide__row" id="protein"><h2>01 / PROTEIN</h2><div>
  <p class="inchy-kicker">HUMAN MEAL STUDY</p>
  <h3>PROTEIN AND YOUR BODY’S OWN SIGNALS</h3>
  <p>In a randomized crossover study, eight healthy volunteers ate breakfasts high in protein, carbohydrate or fat, matched for calories, volume and appearance. The high-protein breakfast produced higher GLP-1 and PYY concentrations.</p>
  <p>In everyday terms: changing what a meal contains can change the gut-hormone response after eating. In this small study, the hormone differences did not lead to a significant difference in how much participants ate at the next meal.</p>
  <p class="inchy-small">Van der Klaauw et al. · Obesity · 2013 (published online 2012). The study examined meals, not sacha inchi or finished INCHY.</p>
  <a class="inchy-text-link" href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/oby.20154">READ THE PROTEIN STUDY →</a>
</div></section>
<section class="inchy-guide__row" id="fiber"><h2>02 / FIBER</h2><div>
  <p class="inchy-kicker">REVIEW OF HUMAN TRIALS</p>
  <h3>ANOTHER PART OF THE SATIETY PICTURE</h3>
  <p>Gut bacteria can ferment certain dietary fibers into short-chain fatty acids. These can signal to cells in the gut to release GLP-1. Fiber types differ, so their effects cannot be treated as interchangeable.</p>
  <p>A 2026 scoping review examined 52 studies involving 1,085 participants. Some fibers increased GLP-1, but effects varied and greater satiety did not consistently accompany a hormone increase. Many studies were small and short.</p>
  <p>Water-holding capacity, viscosity and fermentability describe different properties. Holding water is not, by itself, evidence that a fiber stimulates GLP-1. This review does not establish the response to INCHY’s fiber or show that protein and fiber have equal effects.</p>
  <p class="inchy-small">De Jong et al. · Frontiers in Endocrinology · 2026. Research on specified fiber preparations, not a trial of INCHY.</p>
  <a class="inchy-text-link" href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/endocrinology/articles/10.3389/fendo.2026.1880500/full">READ THE FIBER REVIEW →</a>
</div></section>
<section class="inchy-guide__row" id="peptides"><h2>03 / SACHA<br>INCHI PEPTIDES</h2><div>
  <p class="inchy-kicker">LABORATORY RESEARCH / IN VITRO</p>
  <h3>RESEARCH SHOWS</h3>
  <p>Researchers isolated peptides—small protein fragments—from hydrolyzed sacha inchi meal. Several inhibited DPP-IV in laboratory assays.</p>
  <h3 class="inchy-spaced-heading">WHY IT MATTERS</h3>
  <p>DPP-IV is an enzyme that breaks down GLP-1. This research concerns the enzyme involved in that breakdown, a different question from stimulating GLP-1 release.</p>
  <h3 class="inchy-spaced-heading">WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?</h3>
  <p>The tested peptides slowed the enzyme in a laboratory setting. This suggests a possible mechanism for further investigation; it does not show that consuming INCHY keeps GLP-1 active longer in the body.</p>
  <h3 class="inchy-spaced-heading">WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE</h3>
  <p>This was not a human trial of finished INCHY. The preparations and concentrations tested cannot be assumed to match a serving of the product.</p>
  <p class="inchy-small">Zhang et al. · Journal of the Science of Food and Agriculture · 2023.<br>Isolation and identification of dipeptidyl peptidase-IV inhibitory peptides from Sacha inchi meal.</p>
  <div class="inchy-guide__links"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36692392/">Read the PubMed abstract</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jsfa.12464">Read the publisher’s article</a></div>
</div></section>
<section class="inchy-guide__row" id="inchy-evidence"><h2>WHAT THIS MEANS<br>FOR INCHY</h2><div>
  <p>INCHY is a sacha inchi nutraceutical focused on satiety. Our proprietary process keeps protein and fiber from the same seed together in a functional ingredient designed for everyday foods and drinks.</p>
  <p>The research explains the scientific interest in these components. It does not establish that a serving of INCHY increases GLP-1, prevents cravings or causes weight loss. Demonstrating those outcomes would require evidence relevant to the finished ingredient and serving size.</p>
  <p>Keeping protein and fiber together is a feature of our ingredient. It is not evidence, on its own, of greater satiety per calorie or a stronger effect than taking protein and fiber separately.</p>
